  "id": "COMPOUND:31777 - PROTEIN:585",
  "verbalization": "Tatridin B is implicated in the inhibition of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) through a multi-step mechanism involving the enzyme Ectonucleotide pyrophosphatase/phosphodiesterase family member 6. This pathway is supported by three distinct evidence chains, each linking Tatridin B to GAPDH via different intermediates. The first evidence chain directly connects Tatridin B to GAPDH through a known target, while the second provides a complex, high-scoring pathway involving a synthetic compound that also targets GAPDH. The third evidence chain links Tatridin B to N-methylnicotinamide, which in turn targets GAPDH, further reinforcing the role of this enzyme in the pathway.\n\nThe integration of these pathways suggests that Tatridin B may act as a competitive inhibitor of GAPDH, potentially through a mechanism involving the modulation of the Ectonucleotide pyrophosphatase/phosphodiesterase family member 6, which is known to regulate the activity of GAPDH. The presence of multiple targets and the high scores in the evidence chains indicate a plausible and well-supported mechanism, though the exact nature of the interaction between Tatridin B and the enzyme remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a direct and indirect relationship between Tatridin B and GAPDH, with the enzyme playing a central role in the pathway.",
